#~ msgid "Nothing backs up your data automatically. In the Oracle console, under Block Storage, you can take a backup of the server's boot volume at any time. The free tier includes five volume backups, so take them by hand and delete old ones rather than attaching a scheduled policy, which would keep more than five and be charged."
#~ msgstr ""

#: ../portal/src/routes/docs/installation/oracle-cloud/+page.svelte
msgid "No domain yet? Get one from DuckDNS"
msgstr ""

#~ msgid "<0>DuckDNS</0> is a free dynamic DNS service that gives out names under <1>duckdns.org</1>, each with a wildcard for the subdomains below it. The installer uses your DuckDNS token to point your name at your server, so there is nothing to set up by hand."
#~ msgstr ""

#: ../portal/src/routes/docs/installation/oracle-cloud/+page.svelte
msgid "For a domain you already own, you add two records by hand at your DNS provider in <0>Step 3</0>."
msgstr ""

#~ msgid "Oracle switches off servers on Always Free accounts that it judges idle for seven days in a row. Its measure is whether the processor, network and memory each stayed below 20 percent of capacity for nearly all of that week. A Nocturne server for one person does very little work by that yardstick, so left alone it is likely to be stopped after its first quiet week. There are two ways to prevent that, and one of them changes the command in <0>Step 2</0>, so pick now rather than later."
#~ msgstr ""

#~ msgid "Pay As You Go suits most people: it is a single change in the Oracle console, adds nothing to the server, and paying accounts are served first when Ampere capacity is scarce. The trade-off is that the card already on file <0>can</0> be charged if anything outside the free allowance is ever created. Choose Folding@home instead if you would rather keep an Always Free account that cannot be billed at all, and you do not mind a second piece of software with network access on the server that holds your health data."
#~ msgstr ""

#~ msgid "Paste this into Cloud Shell, replacing <0/> with your domain, such as <1/>. Nocturne will answer on that name and every site you create gets a subdomain under it, so pick something you are happy to keep. If you chose Folding@home above, add <2/> in front."
#~ msgstr ""

#~ msgid "If your domain is under duckdns.org, the installer asks early on for your DuckDNS token. <0>Nothing appears on screen as you paste it</0> – the token is hidden, and the installer tells you how many characters it read so you can see the paste landed. If DuckDNS does not accept it you can paste another. Press Enter with nothing pasted and it tells you the address to set yourself in <1>Step 3</1> instead. For any other domain, it prints the two records to create by hand. Either way it keeps working while the server boots, and waits for DNS before it requests certificates."
#~ msgstr ""

#~ msgid "Skip this step if you gave the installer a DuckDNS token. For a DuckDNS name without one, sign in at duckdns.org, put the IP address the script printed into the <0>current ip</0> box next to your name and click <1>update ip</1>. For any other domain, create two <2>A</2> records at your domain provider, pointing at that address:"
